<section>
    <h2>The page is talking behind your back</h2>
    <p>
        Most of what a modern page shows arrives over the network after it loads: JSON from an API, filled into
        the page by JavaScript. Some of what arrives is never shown at all. A test that can read those responses
        can check data the user never sees, find out why a page shows the wrong thing, and wait for exactly the
        call it cares about.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Catch a response as it happens</h2>
    <p>
        <code>page.waitForResponse</code> returns a promise that resolves with the first response that matches.
        Start waiting <em>first</em>, do the thing that sends the request, then <code>await</code> the promise:
    </p>
<pre><code>const searched = page.waitForResponse(r =&gt; r.url().includes('/api/search') &amp;&amp; r.status() === 200);
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Search' }).click();
const response = await searched;</code></pre>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>Order matters.</strong> If you click first and only then start waiting, a fast response can come
        and go before you're listening, and your test waits for something that already happened. Notice there's no
        <code>await</code> on the first line: you want the promise, so you can start the click while it listens.
    </p>
    <p>
        It works for a page load too: start waiting, then navigate, and you'll catch requests the page makes as it
        opens:
    </p>
<pre><code>const profile = page.waitForResponse(r =&gt; r.url().includes('/api/profile'));
await page.goto('/dashboard');
const response = await profile;</code></pre>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Read what came back</h2>
    <p>A response knows its URL, status and headers, and can give you its body:</p>
<pre><code>console.log(`${response.status()} ${response.url()}`);

const json = await response.json();          // the parsed body
const name = json.user.name;

const text = await response.text();          // the raw body, if it isn't JSON</code></pre>
    <p>
        Not sure which request you need? Log them all while you explore:
        <code>page.on('response', r =&gt; console.log(r.status(), r.url()));</code>
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Step into the wire with routes</h2>
    <p>
        Listening is half of it. <code>page.route</code> lets a test answer a request itself: return fake data,
        simulate a failure, or block a slow third-party script. It's how you test the error message a page shows
        when the API is down, without taking the API down.
    </p>
<pre><code>await page.route('**/api/profile', route =&gt; route.fulfill({
  status: 500,
  json: { error: 'boom' },
}));

await page.goto('/dashboard');
await expect(page.getByRole('alert')).toHaveText("Couldn't load your profile");</code></pre>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Listen to the vault</h2>
    <p>
        The vault in this floor's trap room whispers its combination as you walk in, over the wire, and never
        writes it on the wall. What you can see on the page are echoes, and echoes lie. Five seconds.
    </p>
</section>
